## §12 HOST anomalies

1. **Dual-emission pattern for first 51 ticks; single-emit for last 8.** From T=2573 through T=8573 each heartbeat line appears twice at 16-line separation. From T=8693 through T=9533 each heartbeat appears once. This is consistent with a HOST peer running two motor instances simultaneously (ClientServer mode): one server-authoritative motor and one HOST-local client-predicted motor. Around T=8573 one of the two instances ceased emitting — most likely a scene transition / player respawn / component teardown on the HOST-local side. Not a regression: every one of the 110 emitted rows shows div=0, and the unique-tick dedup produces a clean 1:1 active-ticks progression.

2. **HOST imp-compared cadence mismatch between the two emitters.** Within a given tick pair (e.g. T=5213), the two emissions sometimes show different imp-compared values (one shows 1 while the other shows 0, later one shows 7 while the other shows 6, etc.). This is expected: imp-compared is per-motor-scope counter tracking how many impulse reconciles that motor has observed; the server-side and HOST-local-client motors observe impulses on slightly different cadences (real vs replay-window). Each motor's internal imp-div (compared within its own two-scratch) is 0 on every row — no divergence.

3. **hof-compared plateau at 1.** Exactly one handoff consume during the ~70s session (T=4973). Other 58 hof-compared=1 rows represent the post-consume plateau, not additional consumes. Handoff resolver coverage requirement satisfied; no later hof consumes tracked (would require multiple intro→gameplay transitions per session).

4. **imp-compared monotonic growth 0→8 across run.** 6-8 impulses consumed over 58s of active gameplay post-first-impulse (T=5213). Consistent with normal skill usage cadence.

## §13 HOST verdict
